00:13:29.000 --> 00:14:32.000
As a result, the building started before Buttson could figure out the structural basics. Joseph K. Hill laid the foundation stone on the 2nd of March 1959. Within a year, he was dead, killed by his third heart attack. But just as he'd intended, so much work had already been done that stopping the project was unthinkable. By now, an engineer had been found. His name was Over-Arupp, the Anglo-Danish boss of Over Arup and Partners, a respected engineering firm. The two Danes, Utsun and Arup, had been working together to try to figure out a solution to the structural problem. And it was at this point that Arup informed Utsun of the bad news. His beautiful, free-standing roof shells simply couldn't be built.
结果，在乌松弄清楚结构基础之前，建筑工程就已经开始了。1959 年 3 月 2 日，约瑟夫·卡希尔奠定了基石。一年内，他就去世了，死于第三次心脏病发作。但正如他所愿，当时已有太多工作完成，叫停这个项目已是不可想象的事。此时，人们已经找到了一位工程师。他的名字叫奥韦·阿鲁普，是备受尊敬的工程公司“奥韦·阿鲁普合伙人公司”的英籍丹麦裔老板。两位丹麦人乌松和阿鲁普一直在合作试图找出解决结构问题的方案。正是在这个时候，阿鲁普将这个坏消息告诉了乌松：他那美观的、独立的屋顶壳体根本无法建造。

23
00:14:32.000 --> 00:15:20.000
There's no such thing as too many daines, so let's meet a third. Bent Flubia. He's the world's leading expert on large projects, and on why these mega projects are so often delivered late and way over budget. And he's fascinated by the Sydney Opera House because it's the ultimate case study, the definitive fiasco of how not to run a mega project. Flubio says that the original scene of many mega projects is this. People start building before they figure out what they're really trying to do. The ideal mega project starts with a question, why are we doing this? Then there's a long, careful planning process.
世上没有太多的丹麦人，所以让我们来认识第三位：本特·弗利维伯格。他是大型项目以及这些特大型项目为何总是延期且严重超预算的世界顶级专家。他之所以对悉尼歌剧院着迷，是因为它是终极案例研究，是运作特大型项目的绝对反面教材。弗利维伯格表示，许多特大型项目的最初症结在于：人们在弄清楚自己究竟想做什么之前就已经开始建设了。理想的特大型项目始于一个问题：我们为什么要这样做？然后是一个漫长而谨慎的规划过程。

